    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the owner request while keeping the flat `address` fully backward-compatible.\n\n**Request contract (`openapi.yml` \u2192 generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto`)**\n- Added optional `addressLine1` (non-blank when present) and `addressLine2` (optional).\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list (`city`, `telephone`, `firstName`, `lastName` stay required).\n- Added a class-level `@ValidAddressForm` alongside the existing `@ValidPostcodeForCity`.\n\n**New validation** \u2014 `ValidAddressForm` / `ValidAddressFormValidator`: an owner is valid when it supplies an address in *either* form (non-blank `addressLine1` **or** non-blank flat `address`); otherwise a violation is reported on `addressLine1`. `city` remains independently required.\n\n**Address composition** \u2014 new `AddressComposer` util (single responsibility, delegates each part to the existing `AddressNormalizer`): prefers the structured form, returning normalized `addressLine1` with a single space + normalized `addressLine2` appended when present, else falls back to the normalized flat `address`.\n\n**Persistence & mapping**\n- `Owner` entity gained `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns (+ getters/setters); schema added to all four DB dialects (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`).\n- `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` now normalizes each structured line and composes the stored `address` via `AddressComposer`. `toOwnerDto` returns the normalized lines and the composed `address`.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.updateOwner` reuses the same normalize/compose logic so create and update stay consistent.\n\n**On the \"reads the address\" consumers:** the household hash (`lastName`+`postcode`), postcode validation (`city`+`postcode`) and locality (`city`+`postcode`) all read the top-level `city`/`postcode` fields, which are single fields shared by both forms \u2014 so they already operate correctly regardless of which address form was supplied; no change was needed there beyond storing the composed `address`.\n\nThe two acceptance tests (`coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress`, `functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress`) both pass.",
